  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Together Against Poverty-GROW Programme in Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Determined to challenge negative attitudes towards small -scale female food producers, Oxfam began actively partnering with the Ministry of Agriculture, National Media Agency and local partners to implement the Female Food Heroes (FFH) initiative, which has been active since 2012. The FFH concept was developed with a notion that the agriculture sector is underperforming in part because women, who are often a crucial resource in agriculture and the rural economy, face constraints that reduce their productivity. In Ethiopia, the FFH initiative is organized around a contest (&quot;FFH Award&quot;) to find outstanding women farmers &#x2013; ones who have overcome challenges and made significant gains in farming and sustainable agriculture, thus serving as role models in their communities. Their stories are used to promote the status of Ethiopian women food producers and make their working and living conditions understood and visible. Through a variety of activities, the FFH Award program contributes to wider recognition of small -scale female food producers through awarding, acknowledging and celebrating their efforts, creating opportunities for their voices to be heard and to influence decisions and policies that matter to them. Female Food Heroes, and the network that binds them, become involved in advocating on a variety of topics, such as access to land, credit, labour and time -saving agricultural technologies and appropriate budget provisions for women -related investments that deal with gender and food security issues. Ultimately, the FFH Award program is building demand for an enabling environment for women food producers to be at the centre of future debates and solutions.  A key strategy and approach of the FFH program is to sensitize the wider public to bring about eventual attitude changes towards small -scale female food producers and conservation agriculture through media engagement and platforms. By publicly awarding, acknowledging and celebrating female small -scale food produ</narrative>
  </description>
